'''2.''' Yes; yea; even so; truly: indicating assent to what has been said, and introducing a further or stronger statement.
{{smaller block|<poem>What! am I not your king?
If ''ay'', then am I not to be obey’d?</poem>
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', Philaster, iv. 2.}}
The champions, ''ay'', and exemplars too, of classical learning. {{right|''Story'', Speech, Cambridge, Aug. 31, 1826.}}}}
'''3.''' Indeed: suggesting slight surprise, interrogation, anger, or reproach, or simple attention, according to the mode of pronunciation. {{smaller|—'''Aye, aye,''' ''naut.'': (''a'') The phrase by which comprehension of an order is expressed on board ship. (''b'') An answer to a sentry’s hail or to a call.}}

'''aye<sup>3</sup>, ay<sup>3</sup>''' (äi or ī), ''n.'' [< ''aye'', ''adv.'' or ''interj.''] An affirmative answer or vote in deliberative bodies.{{smaller|—'''The ayes and noes,''' the yeas and nays. (''a'') The affirmative and negative votes. (''b'') Those who so vote. Hence—'''The ayes have it,''' in deliberative bodies, the phrase employed by the presiding officer in declaring that the affirmative votes are in a majority.}}
{{smaller block|Another was the late Speaker Trevor, who had, from the chair, put the question, whether he was or was not a rogue, and had been forced to pronounce that ''the Ayes had it.''
{{right|''Macaulay'', Hist. Eng., xxiii.}}}}

'''aye<sup>4</sup>†''', ''n.'' See ''ey''<sup>1</sup>.

'''aye-aye''' (ī′ī), ''n.'' [< F. ''aye-aye'', < Malagasy ''aiay'', also dial. ''ahay'', ''haihay'', prob. of imitative origin (cf. ''ai'' and ''ai-ai''). Reduplication is characteristic of imitative names, particularly in native languages.] A name of a remarkable lemurine

